 "verdict": "ACCOUNTING (no rule tested): T1 pooled 2007-26 stays positive with month t ≥ 2 at measured demo costs (+0.147R, t +6.85) and does not at pessimistic costs + 0.10 slippage (+0.036R, t +1.64)",
 "reading": "No rule was tested; the same 3713 pooled trades were charged under each cost assumption. Before costs T1 makes +0.155R a trade pooled 2007-26 (gross month t +7.24); it breaks even at $1.14 a round trip and keeps month t 2 up to $0.81 (8.18 bp / 5.85 bp of price). By window, t-2 cost: 2007-11 $0.81, 2012-15 $0.48, 2016-20 $0.19, 2021-26 $0.54. At the measured demo cost (0.42 bp of price, no slippage) pooled +0.147R (t +6.85); with 0.10 × M slippage +0.133R (t +6.18). At the studies' $0.60 +0.073R (t +3.35). Pessimistic by era with 0.10 × M +0.036R (t +1.64); 2007-11 +0.040R, 2016-20 −0.020R. Cautions, said plainly: the demo figure is one day of quotes on one demo account in November 2026 and carries no gold slippage measurement; it is applied to 2007-26 as a share of price, which assumes today's relative spread held then. At 2007 prices that share is about $0.03 a round trip, far under any retail gold spread of the time; the same figure in today's dollars (route D$) gives +0.141R (t +6.56). The era spreads are assumptions, not measurements. The thinnest window is 2016-20 (gross t +2.87, month t 2 only up to $0.19 a round trip): it is flat or negative at $0.60 and under P. Fills in Asia hours (985 of 3713) carry most of the gross (+0.383R a trade against +0.047R for New York fills), and Asia is where spreads are widest, which no route here charges separately. Whether T1 is worth anything therefore turns on the cost actually paid: an all-in round trip (slippage included) under $0.81 keeps the pooled month t over 2, under $0.48-0.54 keeps it in 2012-15 and 2021-26, and 2016-20 needs under $0.19. Nothing live changes; a forward paper record is the owner's decision."
